  "notes": "The kiosk on the repeating arrival road, on a 1988 network: a grey pay-on-answer coinbox with a rotary dial (docs/plans/phone-box-1988.md). src/game/payphone.js picks the node. Important calls (the key, the sheet and the loop) come in and need only receiver pickup. The rotary remains optional. Outgoing calls are pay on answer: when the far end picks up he hears rapid pips until the slot is fed; he has no coins, and the tape fits, and the call records over his takes. Numbers are found, never remembered for him: the van's car phone on the dash, Lyle's he still knows (his old hall payphone), Sarah's on the torn directory page, her sister's in the phone book and from 192. Calling himself escalates by pass: the van rings early; on the loop pass the box rings until he lifts the receiver; late, he is already on the line. Lines marked here carry weight and are Seb's to revise first: lyle-nobody, sarah, sarah-confessed, loop-spoken, self-line, self-line-mic. THE PAGES (docs/plans/outskirts-losing-himself.md): flurry, flurry.lyle, flurry.them, flurry.him are read by pass, Sarah's first and his last; his worst moments, both sides, everyone's. All drafts for Seb; the lines that have to land are \"there's always another take\" and \"That was all you got.\" The late self-call is his box heard from underwater (sound self-underwater).",
